Although I said I don't intend to join the discussion, I change my mind=20
because I observed on bpfk-list a movement toward modifying the grammar=20
around lerfu and numerals.=20
https://groups.google.com/d/topic/bpfk-list/GQ9Mnwieue0/discussion

First, creating a convention for a sumti in {de'i/ti'u/te'i} clause is not=
=20
at all interest me. If we need, we can always specify the structure and=20
meaning of sumti by a {vede'i/veti'u} clause according to official English=
=20
definition, or by a {tede'i/teti'u/tete'i} clause according to experimental=
=20
Lojban definition.

Secondly, removing {pi'e}s in li clause is nonsense. Even though {pi'e}=20
separates digits, it denotes one number, on which carrying may occur across=
=20
{pi'e} in calculation. For example, {de'i li 2015 pi'e 5 pi'e 31 su'i=20
pi'epi'e 1} should mean the same date as {de'i li 2015 pi'e 6 pi'e 1}. In=
=20
usual mathematical notation and in ISO_8601, a number is represented in the=
=20
form "higher column to the left". In the case of date, a number [ny:ly:dy]=
=20
means [365*nyxipy+366*nyxiky]+[29*lyxity+30*lyxixy+31*lyxizy]+dy, where the=
=20
bases [365/366] [29/30/31] should vary according to a complicated but=20
unique rule. You may change the order of columns "{vede'i lo drata be=20
I'ySyO'y xi 8601}" if you want.

Thirdly, You don't need selma'o LI if you remove {pi'e}: removing {pi'e}=20
breaks separation of digits on different bases. The resulting string of=20
numerals and lerfu is out of mathematical rule. If you once abandon=20
carrying of calculation, you can use raw lerfu as sumti in arbitrary order:=
=20
{de'i jydyxi2 joi lyxi5 joi dyxi26 joi nyxi2015}. There are no need of=20
modifying grammar.

Sorry for bothering, but I don't understand the reason for modifying=20
grammar for such an indifferent-to-me matter. I would be in favor of=20
modifying grammar if any more important requirement occurred, though,
=20

